  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe story today and for the next few days, as it has been for \nseemingly forever (actually the past two and a half months) is \nmonsoonal moisture. Satellite imagery shows an area of moisture \nadvancing northward with showers and embedded thunder moving toward \nSweetwater County as I write this around 1 am. This area of \nshowers will spread northward tonight. Meanwhile, most of \nnorthern Wyoming should remain dry tonight.\n\nThis deeper moisture will overspread the entire area today, with \nprecipitable water levels rising to 150 to 175 percent of normal. \nJust about all areas will have at least a 1 in 6 chance of a shower \nor thunderstorm today. The best chances though will be across \nwestern Wyoming where there will be more forcing, and also across \nareas East of the Big Horns, with the least chance in the center, \nespecially the central basins. There continues to be one factor that \nmakes me think coverage of thunderstorms may be less though. \nThere will be a decent amount of cloud cover around the area, \nand this could limit surface instability somewhat. And it is we \nare now almost to the autumnal equinox, not the middle of \nsummer. So, the sun is weaker and daytime heating will be less. \nThis may also limit the chance of heavy rain and any flooding \npotential. Steering flow is less than yesterday, but we still \nhave 10 to 20 knots and this should be enough to keep storms \nmoving somewhat. However, with the high precipitable waters \nthere could still be isolated minor flooding, especially west of\nthe Continental Divide. Most showers should end after sunset, \nbut a few showers will likely linger through the night. And it \nwill probably be more than expected, the models have been \nhorrible this season with nocturnal monsoonal convection.\n\nAn upper level low that has been helping to funnel the moisture into \nthe area will finally begin to move eastward today, drifting \ntoward Idaho by tomorrow. This may be the most active day for \nconvection of the period, especially thunderstorms. Most \nguidance is showing a clearer sky in the morning, allowing for \nmore daytime heating and surface instability. Instability \nparameters are decent for this time of year, with CAPE \napproaching 700 J/Kg and lifted indices as low as minus 4. With \nthe approaching upper level flow, there will also be some decent\ndirectional shear, especially in western Wyoming. The result \nwill be a chance for some stronger thunderstorms, again \nespecially in western Wyoming where there is a marginal risk of \nsevere weather. The main threat would be hail given the low wet \nbulb zero levels. With more sun and southwest flow continuing, \nthis will also be a warmer day, especially East of the Divide \nwhere warmer spots may get close to 80 for a high. Showers will \ncontinue into the night as well. With the added instability and \na shortwave rotating around the low, if there are any flooding \nproblems, this would be the time for it.\n\nThe latest guidance continues to slow the progression of the low, \nonly slowly moving it over Wyoming by Saturday. On this day, \nthe best chance of showers and storms would be across northern \nWyoming. Most guidance is in decent agreement on this. The \ndeepest moisture would be over Montana, but most areas will \nhave a chance. There may also be a dry slot that moves in \nportions of the area, but exactly where is still in question. \nThe low will slowly cross the state then on Sunday, but will \nstill be close enough for some wrap around moisture to bring \nmore showers, mainly to northern and eastern portions of the \narea. This also looks like a cooler day as flow turns to the \nnorthwest, with temperatures anywhere from 5 to 10 degrees \ncooler than on Saturday. Following this, there is more \ndivergence in the models with some bringing another system \nacross the area early next week with another chance of showers \nand others suppressing it to the south, keeping the area largely\nrain free. All guidance shows south to southwesterly flow \nreturning though, so temperatures look to be back above normal \nas we head into the start of autumn, which arrives on Tuesday, \nSeptember 22nd at 6:04 pm.",
